# EphCore.Corrections.ApparentPlace — Light-Time & Aberration

```elixir
Mix.install(
  [
    {:eph_core, path: Path.expand("../..", __DIR__)},
    {:kino, "~> 0.14"},
    {:kino_vega_lite, "~> 0.1"}
  ],
  config: [
    eph_core: [
      # Planetary DE440s only. Avoid config_path: :eph_core here — that loads
      # config.exs asteroid kernels before SPK.Server starts.
      kernel_base_dir: Path.expand("../../priv", __DIR__),
      spk_kernels: ["de440s.bsp"]
    ]
  ]
)

Logger.configure(level: :warning)
```

## Overview

Where a planet *is* and where you *see* it are two different places. The
snapshot pipeline's default output is the **geometric** (astrometric) position —
the true instantaneous geometry. `EphCore.Corrections.ApparentPlace` adds the
two effects that turn geometry into **apparent place**, the position an observer
actually measures and that published almanacs quote:

1. **Light-time retardation** — you see the body where it *was* when the light
   left it, `τ = range / c` ago. The module iterates the retarded epoch
   `jd_tt − τ` (two rounds converge well inside a second).
2. **Annual aberration** — Earth's ~30 km/s orbital motion tilts every incoming
   ray toward the direction of travel by up to the aberration constant,
   `κ = |v| / c ≈ 20.5″`. The port uses Skyfield's exact relativistic
   `add_aberration` formula so the two engines agree.

This is the backbone of the 1-second-accurate almanac (`../02_applications/01_almanac.livemd`).

**Usage shape:** build a per-timestamp `frame/2` once (Earth state, velocity,
nutation, observer geometry), then call `look/2` or `apparent_lon_lat/2` per body.

**How to open:** Install [Livebook](https://livebook.dev/), open this `.livemd`,
evaluate the setup cell. Kernels must be under `eph_core/priv/ephemeris/`.

## Helpers

```elixir
alias VegaLite, as: Vl
alias EphCore.Corrections.ApparentPlace
alias EphCore.Ephemeris
alias EphCore.Geometry.Ecliptic
alias EphCore.SnapshotPipeline
alias AstroUtils.{Angle, Vector}

read_utc = fn input ->
  case Kino.Input.read(input) do
    %DateTime{} = dt -> DateTime.truncate(dt, :second)
    %NaiveDateTime{} = ndt -> ndt |> DateTime.from_naive!("Etc/UTC") |> DateTime.truncate(:second)
  end
end

arcsec = fn deg -> deg * 3600.0 end
:ok
```

## Ready check

```elixir
Kino.Markdown.new("""
**EphCore** `#{Application.spec(:eph_core, :vsn)}` loaded.

Speed of light used by the pass: **#{Float.round(ApparentPlace.c_km_per_day() / 86_400.0, 3)} km/s**
(#{ApparentPlace.c_km_per_day()} km/day).
""")
```

## 1 — Observer & instant → the frame

`frame/2` does all the per-timestamp work once. Everything below reuses it.

```elixir
lat_in = Kino.Input.number("Latitude (°)", default: 44.9778)
lon_in = Kino.Input.number("Longitude (°)", default: -93.2650)
height_in = Kino.Input.number("Height (m)", default: 250)
utc_in = Kino.Input.utc_datetime("UTC", default: ~N[2026-07-21 04:00:00])

Kino.Layout.grid([lat_in, lon_in, height_in, utc_in], columns: 2)
```

```elixir
observer = %{
  lat: Kino.Input.read(lat_in),
  lon: Kino.Input.read(lon_in),
  height: Kino.Input.read(height_in)
}

utc = read_utc.(utc_in)
frame = ApparentPlace.frame(utc, observer)

v_mag = Vector.magnitude(frame.earth_velocity_km_per_day)
# Aberration constant κ = |v|/c, expressed in arcseconds.
kappa_arcsec = v_mag / ApparentPlace.c_km_per_day() * Angle.rad_to_deg(1.0) * 3600.0

Kino.Markdown.new("""
| Frame field | Value |
|-------------|-------|
| JD(TT) | #{Float.round(frame.jd_tt, 6)} |
| LAST (local apparent sidereal time) | #{Float.round(frame.last_deg, 4)}° |
| Earth barycentric speed | #{Float.round(v_mag / 86_400.0, 3)} km/s |
| Aberration constant κ = speed ÷ c | **#{Float.round(kappa_arcsec, 2)}″** |

The classical aberration constant is ~20.5″; Earth's speed varies slightly over
the year (perihelion in January), so κ breathes with it.
""")
```

## 2 — Geometric vs apparent, per body

For each body we compute the **geometric** geocentric ecliptic position (straight
from the ephemeris) and the **apparent** geocentric position (light-time +
aberration), then measure the angular separation.

```elixir
bodies = [:mercury, :venus, :mars, :jupiter, :saturn, :uranus, :neptune, :moon]

geo_vs_app =
  for body <- bodies do
    # Geometric: raw geocentric vector → true-of-date ecliptic lon/lat.
    {:ok, state} = Ephemeris.geocentric_state(frame.jd_tt, body)
    {geo_lon, geo_lat} = Ecliptic.icrf_to_true_lon_lat(state.geocentric_position_km, frame.jd_tt)

    # Apparent geocentric: light-time + aberration.
    %{geocentric: {app_lon, app_lat}} = ApparentPlace.apparent_lon_lat(frame, body)

    dlon = Angle.signed_delta(geo_lon, app_lon)
    dlat = app_lat - geo_lat
    total = :math.sqrt(dlon * dlon * :math.pow(:math.cos(Angle.deg_to_rad(geo_lat)), 2) + dlat * dlat)

    light_min = state.geocentric_range_km / (ApparentPlace.c_km_per_day() / 86_400.0) / 60.0

    %{
      body: body,
      range_Mkm: Float.round(state.geocentric_range_km / 1_000_000, 2),
      light_time_min: Float.round(light_min, 2),
      geometric_lon: Float.round(geo_lon, 5),
      apparent_lon: Float.round(app_lon, 5),
      shift_arcsec: Float.round(arcsec.(total), 2)
    }
  end

Kino.DataTable.new(geo_vs_app, name: "Geometric vs apparent geocentric")
```

```elixir
Vl.new(width: 560, height: 300, title: "Total geometric→apparent shift (arcsec)")
|> Vl.data_from_values(geo_vs_app)
|> Vl.mark(:bar)
|> Vl.encode_field(:x, "body", type: :nominal, sort: Enum.map(geo_vs_app, &to_string(&1.body)))
|> Vl.encode_field(:y, "shift_arcsec", type: :quantitative, title: "Shift (″)")
|> Vl.encode_field(:color, "light_time_min", type: :quantitative,
  scale: [scheme: "magma"], title: "Light time (min)")
```

Notice the shift clusters near the ~20″ aberration constant for most bodies —
aberration dominates. Fast, nearby bodies (the Moon, inner planets) add a
larger light-time term on top, because they move appreciably during the delay.

## 3 — Anatomy: light-time delay

Light-time is pure geometry: `τ = range / c`. Sunlight-on-a-planet takes minutes
to hours to reach us; over that delay a fast body slides measurably along its path.

```elixir
lt_rows =
  for body <- [:moon, :mars, :jupiter, :saturn, :neptune] do
    {:ok, state} = Ephemeris.geocentric_state(frame.jd_tt, body)
    c_km_s = ApparentPlace.c_km_per_day() / 86_400.0
    tau_s = state.geocentric_range_km / c_km_s

    %{
      body: body,
      range_Mkm: Float.round(state.geocentric_range_km / 1_000_000, 2),
      light_time: cond do
        tau_s < 90 -> "#{Float.round(tau_s, 1)} s"
        tau_s < 5400 -> "#{Float.round(tau_s / 60, 2)} min"
        true -> "#{Float.round(tau_s / 3600, 2)} h"
      end
    }
  end

Kino.DataTable.new(lt_rows, name: "Light-time delay by body")
```

## 4 — Anatomy: aberration direction

Aberration always tilts the apparent position **toward** the point Earth is
racing toward (the apex of its orbital motion). We can see this by comparing the
apparent shift's direction to Earth's velocity vector projected onto the sky.

```elixir
# Earth's velocity direction in ecliptic longitude (the "apex" of motion).
{vx, vy, vz} = frame.earth_velocity_km_per_day
{apex_lon, apex_lat} = Ecliptic.icrf_to_true_lon_lat({vx, vy, vz}, frame.jd_tt)

Kino.Markdown.new("""
**Apex of Earth's motion** (where aberration points, in true-of-date ecliptic):

* longitude **#{Float.round(apex_lon, 2)}°**, latitude **#{Float.round(apex_lat, 2)}°**
* magnitude of the tilt at the apex ≈ κ = **#{Float.round(v_mag / ApparentPlace.c_km_per_day() * Angle.rad_to_deg(1.0) * 3600.0, 2)}″**

A body 90° from the apex gets the full κ tilt; a body toward or away from the
apex is displaced radially (foreshortened), not sideways.
""")
```

## 5 — Topocentric parallax: the Moon steals the show

`apparent_lon_lat/2` returns both the **geocentric** apparent place and the
**topocentric** one (observer position subtracted). The gap is diurnal parallax
— tiny for planets, but nearly a degree for the Moon.

```elixir
parallax_rows =
  for body <- [:moon, :venus, :mars, :jupiter, :saturn] do
    %{geocentric: {glon, glat}, topocentric: {tlon, tlat}} =
      ApparentPlace.apparent_lon_lat(frame, body)

    dlon = Angle.signed_delta(glon, tlon)
    dlat = tlat - glat
    sep = :math.sqrt(dlon * dlon + dlat * dlat)

    %{
      body: body,
      geocentric_lon: Float.round(glon, 5),
      topocentric_lon: Float.round(tlon, 5),
      parallax_arcsec: Float.round(arcsec.(sep), 1)
    }
  end

Kino.DataTable.new(parallax_rows, name: "Geocentric vs topocentric (diurnal parallax)")
```

The Moon's parallax runs to thousands of arcseconds (up to ~1°) — which is why
lunar rise/set times are meaningfully observer-dependent.

## 6 — Apparent look angles (alt/az/HA/dec)

`look/2` returns the fully-corrected topocentric horizon coordinates — the same
values the almanac's rise/set/transit solver brackets.

```elixir
look_rows =
  for body <- [:sun, :moon, :mercury, :venus, :mars, :jupiter, :saturn] do
    l = ApparentPlace.look(frame, body)

    %{
      body: body,
      altitude_deg: Float.round(l.alt_deg, 3),
      azimuth_deg: Float.round(l.az_deg, 3),
      hour_angle_deg: Float.round(l.hour_angle_deg, 3),
      declination_deg: Float.round(l.declination_deg, 3),
      range_Mkm: Float.round(l.range_km / 1_000_000, 3)
    }
  end

Kino.DataTable.new(look_rows, name: "Apparent topocentric look angles")
```

## 7 — Cross-check against the snapshot pipeline

The pipeline can produce apparent geocentric longitude directly via correction
flags. Our `ApparentPlace` output should match it to well under an arcsecond.

```elixir
check_rows =
  for body <- [:mars, :jupiter, :saturn, :neptune] do
    {:ok, o} =
      SnapshotPipeline.observe(utc, observer, [body],
        corrections: %{light_time: true, aberration: true}
      )

    pipeline_lon = o.solar_system_positions[body].apparent_geocentric_ecliptic_longitude
    %{geocentric: {app_lon, _}} = ApparentPlace.apparent_lon_lat(frame, body)

    %{
      body: body,
      apparent_place_lon: Float.round(app_lon, 8),
      pipeline_lon: Float.round(pipeline_lon, 8),
      diff_arcsec: Float.round(arcsec.(abs(Angle.signed_delta(app_lon, pipeline_lon))), 5)
    }
  end

Kino.DataTable.new(check_rows, name: "ApparentPlace vs SnapshotPipeline")
```

## Pitfalls & notes

1. **Geometric is the pipeline default** — `observe/4` returns astrometric
   positions unless you pass `corrections: %{light_time: true, aberration: true}`.
   Apparent place is opt-in.

2. **Gravitational light deflection is omitted** — it's < 0.05″ except grazing
   the Sun's limb, so `ApparentPlace` drops it. That's the only term separating
   this from a full relativistic reduction.

3. **The diurnal aberration term (~0.3″) is dropped** — only Earth's *orbital*
   (annual) velocity is used. For planets this is negligible; for the strictest
   stellar work it isn't.

4. **`frame/2` is the reuse unit** — building it re-sums the 106-term IAU 2000A
   nutation and re-queries Earth's state. Pass `:nutation` / `:earth_velocity`
   to reuse them across a tight time bracket (the almanac's hot-path trick).

5. **Near a station, arcseconds become hours** — a few arcseconds of apparent
   longitude offset maps to hours of event-timing error when a planet is nearly
   stationary. See `../02_applications/04_retrograde_stations.livemd`.

## Key ideas

* **You never see *now*** — light-time means every planet in the sky is a little
  bit in the past; Neptune is a four-hour-old photograph.
* **Earth's speed bends the sky ~20″** — aberration is the same effect as rain
  slanting on a moving car's windshield, scaled to `v/c` and the whole sky.
* **Compute once, look many** — `frame/2` amortizes the expensive per-instant work
  so `look/2` per body is cheap. This is why the almanac can afford a
  Skyfield-grade reduction at every root-finding step.
